::ENG1_U01_L07_Q01::[MLA.ENG1.R.10; MLA.ENG1.V.01] Passage: The school added a digital board near the entrance. It displayed announcements, bus changes, and club updates in one place. Question: Which response best integrates paraphrase and vocabulary?{
~A. The board was good because it had stuff.#Incorrect. This is too vague.
=B. The digital board organized important updates so students could find information in one place.#Correct. This paraphrases accurately and uses precise vocabulary.
~C. Students entered because announcements existed.#Incorrect. This changes the meaning.
~D. The digital board updated bus club entrance.#Incorrect. This is not a clear sentence.
}

::ENG1_U01_L07_Q02::[MLA.ENG1.R.10; MLA.ENG1.V.01] Passage: The teacher posted vocabulary cards beside the passage. Students used the cards to understand difficult words before answering questions. Question: Which evidence best supports the paraphrase "The cards helped students understand the passage"?{
=A. Students used the cards to understand difficult words.#Correct. This directly supports the paraphrase.
~B. The teacher posted cards.#Incorrect. This states the action but not the result.
~C. The cards were beside the passage.#Incorrect. This gives location but not how they helped.
~D. Students answered questions.#Incorrect. This is too general.
}

::ENG1_U01_L07_Q03::[MLA.ENG1.R.10; MLA.ENG1.V.01] Passage: The response says, "The chart clarified the schedule by showing dates, rooms, and deadlines." Question: Which word is the strongest academic vocabulary?{
~A. says#Incorrect. Says is a common verb.
~B. rooms#Incorrect. Rooms is a detail, not the strongest academic vocabulary.
~C. schedule#Incorrect. Schedule is a topic word here.
=D. clarified#Correct. Clarified is academic vocabulary meaning made clearer.
}

::ENG1_U01_L07_Q04::[MLA.ENG1.R.10; MLA.ENG1.V.01] Passage: The library created a return shelf for reserved books. Students picked up books quickly without waiting in the regular checkout line. Question: Which response is strongest?{
~A. The shelf was by books and students were quick.#Incorrect. This is unclear.
~B. Students waited because the shelf was regular.#Incorrect. This reverses the meaning.
=C. The return shelf made book pickup more efficient because students did not wait in the regular line.#Correct. This uses accurate paraphrase, evidence, and academic vocabulary.
~D. The library had shelves and books.#Incorrect. This is too general.
}

::ENG1_U01_L07_Q05::[MLA.ENG1.R.10; MLA.ENG1.V.01] Passage: The teacher gave a sample paragraph with a topic sentence, evidence, and explanation. Students used it before writing their own responses. Question: Which paraphrase is accurate?{
=A. The sample paragraph helped students see how to organize their own written responses.#Correct. This restates the main idea accurately.
~B. Students copied the sample instead of writing responses.#Incorrect. The passage does not say students copied it.
~C. The teacher removed evidence from the paragraph.#Incorrect. Evidence was included in the sample.
~D. Students wrote before seeing the sample paragraph.#Incorrect. The passage says they used the sample before writing.
}

::ENG1_U01_L07_Q06::[MLA.ENG1.R.10; MLA.ENG1.V.01] Passage: The cafeteria labeled vegetarian meals on the menu. Students could identify those choices quickly. Question: Which academic word best fits the passage?{
~A. confuse#Incorrect. The labels reduced confusion.
~B. hide#Incorrect. The labels made choices visible.
~C. guess#Incorrect. Students did not need to guess.
=D. identify#Correct. Identify means recognize or name, which matches the passage.
}

::ENG1_U01_L07_Q07::[MLA.ENG1.R.10; MLA.ENG1.V.01] Passage: The teacher asked students to underline the sentence that supported their answer. This helped students avoid using interesting details that did not prove their point. Question: Which response best explains the purpose?{
~A. Students underlined any interesting sentence.#Incorrect. The sentence had to support the answer.
=B. Underlining helped students select relevant evidence for their answers.#Correct. This uses academic vocabulary and keeps the meaning accurate.
~C. The teacher wanted longer answers without evidence.#Incorrect. The passage focuses on evidence.
~D. Interesting details always prove a point.#Incorrect. The passage says some interesting details may not prove the point.
}

::ENG1_U01_L07_Q08::[MLA.ENG1.R.10; MLA.ENG1.V.01] Passage: The school website listed club deadlines in one chart. Students used the chart to decide which forms to finish first. Question: Which evidence supports "The chart helped students prioritize forms"?{
~A. The school had a website.#Incorrect. This does not show prioritizing.
~B. The chart listed club deadlines.#Incorrect. This helps, but it does not fully show how students used it.
=C. Students used the chart to decide which forms to finish first.#Correct. This directly supports prioritizing.
~D. Clubs had forms.#Incorrect. This is too general.
}

::ENG1_U01_L07_Q09::[MLA.ENG1.R.10; MLA.ENG1.V.01] Passage: A student says aloud, "The author emphasizes safety by repeating the warning at the beginning and end." Question: What does emphasizes mean?{
~A. hides#Incorrect. Emphasizing does not hide an idea.
=B. gives special importance to#Correct. Repetition makes safety stand out.
~C. removes#Incorrect. The warning is repeated, not removed.
~D. guesses#Incorrect. Emphasizing is not guessing.
}

::ENG1_U01_L07_Q10::[MLA.ENG1.R.10; MLA.ENG1.V.01] Passage: The school posted bus updates on a screen near the office. Students checked the screen before leaving. Question: Which response uses correct conventions and accurate meaning?{
~A. bus updates helped students before leaving#Incorrect. This needs capitalization and punctuation.
~B. Because students checked the screen.#Incorrect. This is a fragment.
~C. The screen helped students it had updates.#Incorrect. This is a run-on.
=D. The screen helped students check bus updates before leaving.#Correct. This is complete, accurate, and correctly punctuated.
}

::ENG1_U01_L07_Q11::[MLA.ENG1.R.10; MLA.ENG1.V.01] Passage: The town added distance markers along the running path. Runners could check how far they had gone without using a phone or watch. Question: Which paraphrase is most accurate?{
=A. The markers helped runners track distance without using another device.#Correct. This keeps the meaning accurate.
~B. Runners stopped using the path because markers were added.#Incorrect. The passage does not say runners stopped.
~C. Phones and watches were required for the markers.#Incorrect. The passage says runners did not need them.
~D. The town removed the running path.#Incorrect. The path remained.
}

::ENG1_U01_L07_Q12::[MLA.ENG1.R.10; MLA.ENG1.V.01] Passage: The response says, "The evidence supports the claim that the signs improved hallway traffic." Question: Which word means backs up or proves?{
~A. evidence#Incorrect. Evidence is the information used to prove an idea.
~B. hallway#Incorrect. Hallway is a place.
~C. traffic#Incorrect. Traffic is the topic, not the action of proving.
=D. supports#Correct. Supports means backs up or proves.
}

::ENG1_U01_L07_Q13::[MLA.ENG1.R.10; MLA.ENG1.V.01] Passage: The science class used a clear jar to model erosion. When water was poured over soil, students watched pieces of dirt move away. Question: Which evidence best supports "The model showed how water can move soil"?{
~A. The jar was clear.#Incorrect. This explains visibility, not the erosion result.
~B. The class was a science class.#Incorrect. This does not support the paraphrase.
=C. Students watched pieces of dirt move away.#Correct. This directly supports the paraphrase.
~D. Water was in a jar.#Incorrect. This is not the strongest evidence.
}

::ENG1_U01_L07_Q14::[MLA.ENG1.R.10; MLA.ENG1.V.01] Passage: The teacher revised the directions by numbering each step. Students followed the process in order and made fewer mistakes. Question: Which academic word best describes what numbering the steps did?{
~A. decorated#Incorrect. Numbering was used for clarity, not decoration.
=B. organized#Correct. Numbering the steps organized the process.
~C. guessed#Incorrect. The steps were clarified, not guessed.
~D. erased#Incorrect. The steps were not erased.
}

::ENG1_U01_L07_Q15::[MLA.ENG1.R.10; MLA.ENG1.V.01] Passage: The teacher asked students to summarize an article by stating the main idea and two important details. Question: What does summarize mean?{
=A. state the main ideas briefly#Correct. This is the meaning of summarize.
~B. copy every word#Incorrect. Summarizing uses fewer words.
~C. add unrelated opinions#Incorrect. A summary should stay connected to the text.
~D. erase the main idea#Incorrect. A summary includes the main idea.
}

::ENG1_U01_L07_Q16::[MLA.ENG1.R.10; MLA.ENG1.V.01] Passage: The response says, "The quiet study room helped students finish homework without hallway noise." Question: Why is this response strong?{
~A. It is long and uses many words.#Incorrect. Length alone does not make it strong.
~B. It avoids the passage meaning.#Incorrect. It uses the passage meaning.
=C. It paraphrases the benefit and includes relevant evidence.#Correct. It explains the benefit and uses the detail about hallway noise.
~D. It has no evidence.#Incorrect. It includes evidence about hallway noise.
}

::ENG1_U01_L07_Q17::[MLA.ENG1.R.10; MLA.ENG1.V.01] Passage: The community garden labeled rows of vegetables. Visitors could identify what was growing without asking a volunteer. Question: Which response is strongest?{
~A. The labels were there and vegetables were growing.#Incorrect. This is vague.
~B. Volunteers asked visitors about labels.#Incorrect. The passage says visitors did not need to ask volunteers.
~C. Vegetables were independent because rows existed.#Incorrect. This is unclear.
=D. The labels helped visitors identify vegetables independently because they did not need to ask a volunteer.#Correct. This integrates paraphrase, vocabulary, and evidence.
}

::ENG1_U01_L07_Q18::[MLA.ENG1.R.10; MLA.ENG1.V.01] Passage: The student writes, "The source is credible because it comes from the school survey." Question: What does credible mean?{
=A. trustworthy#Correct. Credible means trustworthy or believable.
~B. confusing#Incorrect. Credible does not mean confusing.
~C. hidden#Incorrect. Hidden does not mean credible.
~D. unrelated#Incorrect. A credible source can be related and useful.
}

::ENG1_U01_L07_Q19::[MLA.ENG1.R.10; MLA.ENG1.V.01] Passage: The teacher asked students to compare two responses. One used vague words, while the other used specific evidence. Question: Which response uses academic vocabulary correctly?{
~A. The second response is better because it has good stuff.#Incorrect. This is vague.
=B. The second response is stronger because it uses specific evidence.#Correct. This uses academic vocabulary accurately.
~C. The second response specifics evidence betterly.#Incorrect. The words are not used correctly.
~D. The first response is evidence because vague.#Incorrect. This is unclear.
}

::ENG1_U01_L07_Q20::[MLA.ENG1.R.10; MLA.ENG1.V.01] Passage: The museum placed audio guides near the entrance. Visitors listened to explanations while viewing exhibits. Question: Which paraphrase is accurate?{
~A. The museum removed explanations from exhibits.#Incorrect. The audio guides provided explanations.
~B. Visitors could not view exhibits because of audio guides.#Incorrect. They viewed exhibits while listening.
=C. The audio guides helped visitors understand exhibits as they viewed them.#Correct. This keeps the passage meaning.
~D. The entrance listened to the exhibits.#Incorrect. This is not logical.
}

::ENG1_U01_L07_Q21::[MLA.ENG1.R.10; MLA.ENG1.V.01] Passage: The teacher added sentence starters to the discussion board. Students used the starters to explain agreement, disagreement, and evidence more clearly. Question: Which response is correct and precise?{
~A. The starters helped with stuff.#Incorrect. This is vague.
~B. Students explained because board.#Incorrect. This is not a complete sentence.
~C. The discussion was starter evidence agreement.#Incorrect. This is unclear.
=D. Sentence starters helped students explain discussion ideas and evidence more clearly.#Correct. This is accurate, precise, and correctly written.
}

::ENG1_U01_L07_Q22::[MLA.ENG1.R.10; MLA.ENG1.V.01] Passage: The class used a chart to record plant growth each week. Students compared the measurements to see changes over time. Question: Which word best describes what students did with the measurements?{
~A. erased#Incorrect. They did not erase the measurements.
~B. ignored#Incorrect. They used the measurements.
=C. compared#Correct. They looked at measurements to notice changes.
~D. guessed#Incorrect. They used recorded data, not guesses.
}

::ENG1_U01_L07_Q23::[MLA.ENG1.R.10; MLA.ENG1.V.01] Passage: The office created a separate line for students with quick questions. Those students waited less time. Question: Which response best integrates evidence?{
~A. The line helped because it was separate and stuff.#Incorrect. This is vague.
~B. Students had questions, so the office was there.#Incorrect. This does not explain the benefit.
~C. The quick questions waited in the line.#Incorrect. This is illogical.
=D. The separate line helped students with quick questions wait less time.#Correct. This uses the key evidence clearly.
}

::ENG1_U01_L07_Q24::[MLA.ENG1.R.10; MLA.ENG1.V.01] Passage: The cafeteria posted ingredient lists for each meal. Students with allergies could check the lists before choosing lunch. Question: Which academic word best describes the ingredient lists?{
=A. information#Correct. The lists give information students need before choosing lunch.
~B. evidence#Incorrect. The lists can provide information, but in this context the best word is information.
~C. rumor#Incorrect. Ingredient lists are not rumors.
~D. fiction#Incorrect. Ingredient lists are factual, not fiction.
}

::ENG1_U01_L07_Q25::[MLA.ENG1.R.10; MLA.ENG1.V.01] Passage: The response says, "The checklist improved student writing because it reminded students to check evidence, punctuation, and sentence clarity." Question: Which Unit 1 skills are used in this response?{
~A. Only spelling#Incorrect. The response includes more than spelling.
=B. Paraphrase, academic vocabulary, evidence, and conventions#Correct. The response uses all of these Unit 1 skills.
~C. Only speaking#Incorrect. The response is written and evidence-based.
~D. Only unrelated details#Incorrect. The details are relevant.
}
